WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.123 --> 00:00:09.053 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Welcome to Africa episode three of eight. First, I clear the chosen names for
a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:09.703 --> 00:00:21.812 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
We connect three inland countries, Mali, Burkina Faso and Niger, with five
Gulf of Guinea neighbours: Côte d'Ivoire, Ghana, Togo, Benin and Nigeria.

3
00:00:22.484 --> 00:00:26.025 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Mali and its neighbours into view.

4
00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:39.892 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Mali is landlocked, east of Mauritania and north of Guinea, Côte d'Ivoire and
Burkina Faso. Its northern territory reaches deep into the Sahara.

5
00:00:40.552 --> 00:00:52.023 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Timbuktu was a centre of Islamic scholarship, with schools and historic
mosques. Its reputation grew from learning and exchange across the region, not
from being an imaginary distant place.

6
00:00:52.682 --> 00:01:03.688 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Picture a real city of scholars near the Sahara’s southern side. Mali is
inland above the coastal countries, with Niger to its east.

7
00:01:06.047 --> 00:01:15.300 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Burkina Faso is landlocked below Mali and west of Niger. Ghana, Togo, Benin
and Côte d'Ivoire lie toward its southern side.

8
00:01:15.953 --> 00:01:28.446 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The stone walls at Loropéni preserve a fortified settlement linked with the
regional gold trade. The ruins show that West African history includes
substantial stone-built trading centres.

9
00:01:29.097 --> 00:01:40.568 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Picture a stone enclosure above the Gulf of Guinea countries. Burkina Faso has
no coast: Ghana and its neighbours lie between it and the sea.

10
00:01:42.889 --> 00:01:53.257 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Niger is the landlocked country east of Mali and north of Nigeria. Algeria and
Libya lie to its north, with Chad to the east.

11
00:01:53.907 --> 00:02:07.003 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The W-Arly-Pendjari protected landscape crosses Niger, Burkina Faso and Benin.
Its savannah habitats support elephants, lions and other wildlife across
modern borders.

12
00:02:07.668 --> 00:02:18.245 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember a shared savannah park and keep the similar names apart. Niger is the
inland country above Nigeria; Nigeria reaches the Gulf of Guinea.

13
00:02:18.921 --> 00:02:23.182 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Côte d'Ivoire and its neighbours into view.

14
00:02:26.201 --> 00:02:42.025 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Côte d'Ivoire, also called Ivory Coast in English, lies east of Liberia and
west of Ghana. Mali and Burkina Faso are north, while its southern coast faces
the Gulf of Guinea.

15
00:02:42.683 --> 00:02:54.154 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Taï National Park protects tropical forest that is home to pygmy
hippopotamuses and several monkey species. The forest is part of a once more
extensive West African ecosystem.

16
00:02:54.805 --> 00:03:04.743 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Picture a forest hippo on the coast west of Ghana. Côte d'Ivoire reaches the
sea; Burkina Faso, above it, does not.

17
00:03:07.037 --> 00:03:15.141 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Ghana lies between Côte d'Ivoire and Togo, below Burkina Faso. Its southern
edge faces the Gulf of Guinea.

18
00:03:15.801 --> 00:03:29.187 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Fortified trading posts survive along Ghana’s coast, recording centuries of
contact and commerce across the Atlantic. Their history also requires
remembering the human suffering of the slave trade.

19
00:03:29.851 --> 00:03:41.832 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Picture coastal forts between the larger western neighbour and narrow Togo.
Ghana has a southern coast; Burkina Faso sits directly inland to the north.

20
00:03:44.118 --> 00:03:53.290 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Togo is the narrow country between Ghana and Benin. It stretches north from a
short Gulf of Guinea coast toward Burkina Faso.

21
00:03:53.953 --> 00:04:06.492 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
At Koutammakou, Batammariba communities build distinctive earthen tower
houses. The cultural landscape extends across northeastern Togo and
neighbouring Benin, beyond a single national boundary.

22
00:04:07.144 --> 00:04:16.989 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Picture a tower house inside a narrow north-south strip. Togo comes between
Ghana and Benin; both narrow neighbours reach the sea.

23
00:04:19.283 --> 00:04:31.265 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Benin lies east of Togo and west of Nigeria, with Niger and Burkina Faso
toward its north. It narrows toward its Gulf of Guinea coast.

24
00:04:31.923 --> 00:04:44.288 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The royal palaces of Abomey preserve the history of the kingdom of Dahomey.
Successive rulers added palaces within a shared walled area, creating a
layered royal complex.

25
00:04:44.941 --> 00:04:57.050 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember a palace enclosure in the country immediately west of Nigeria. Benin
is a modern country here; the historic Benin City lies in neighbouring
Nigeria.

26
00:04:59.345 --> 00:05:09.585 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Nigeria lies below Niger and west of Cameroon, with Benin on its western side.
Its southern coast opens onto the Gulf of Guinea.

27
00:05:10.248 --> 00:05:22.055 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The Osun-Osogbo Sacred Grove combines forest, a river, shrines and sculpture
in a Yoruba sacred landscape. Nature and cultural practice are closely
connected at this site.

28
00:05:22.717 --> 00:05:33.038 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Picture a sacred forest below Niger. Nigeria is the broad coastal country east
of Benin, while Niger is its landlocked northern neighbour.

29
00:05:40.284 --> 00:05:56.808 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Mali, Burkina Faso and Niger form the inland band. Along the coast, move east
from Côte d'Ivoire to Ghana, Togo, Benin and Nigeria. Niger is above Nigeria;
similar names do not mean the same country.

30
00:05:57.474 --> 00:06:14.320 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember scholarship, stone walls, shared savannah, forest hippos, coastal
forts, tower houses, royal palaces and a sacred grove. Togo and Benin are the
narrow coastal pair between Ghana and Nigeria.
